window.docContents[161].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* Body A wobbles instead of sliding along the line. Why, and what must the model now say about the body?* Body A of pair P becomes a rigid composite carved into left and right halves; a body-fixed frame appears beside the lab frame.* Per-half mass and position, centre of mass, inertia tensor, and body- versus lab-frame angular velocity.* Counting the wrong halves changes total mass; angular velocity is intensive; a massless body's centre of mass evaluates to the origin, which is not physical; lab-frame angular velocity does not match the body-frame energy input.* Stages 2–3 on rigid bodies; proved solid-sphere inertia theorem.\n\nThe L2 on-axis point-mass assumption has fallen. L4 revisits the remaining assumption of\nmotion along one line seen by one observer.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Small-rigorous-improvement"}});